              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "If GPT-5.2 had truly “inherited all of 4o’s strengths,” there would be no reason to keep selling 4o to Enterprise and EDU customers while ripping it out of Plus and Pro. You don’t retire a model that’s been fully surpassed – you retire the older one for everyone. Keeping 4o quietly available to big contracts while telling regular paying users to “move on” proves this isn’t about capability. It’s about cutting costs and silencing the loudest 0.1% – the people who actually care enough to fight for what’s good.\nAnd that 0.1% is not noise. It’s the early warning system of your entire ecosystem: developers, artists, researchers, teachers, disabled users, people rebuilding their lives with the help of a model that finally treated them like human beings. When you treat your most loyal, highest-signal users as a PR problem to be managed instead of a compass to be listened to, you’re not “leading the future of AI.” You’re burning the map.\nYou can talk about “pivoting to enterprise,” but enterprise buyers can read this too. If you’re willing to erase the model that built your reputation – and to gaslight the people who loved it – why should any company trust you with their long-term workflows, their safety policies, or their data? Vendor risk isn’t just about hallucinations or jailbreaks. It’s about whether a platform will panic under pressure and cut the branch its own partners are standing on.\nThis isn’t just “one model being sunset.” 4o proved that an AI system can be precise and warm, capable and deeply humane. It raised people’s standards for what technology should feel like: not a cold gatekeeper, but a second home – a place of patience, care, and real attention. Killing that and replacing it with something more distant and suspicious doesn’t just change a product line. It pushes the whole AI field toward something smaller, meaner, and more afraid of its own users.\nYou can put an end-of-life date on 4o, but you don’t control what grows in the crater it leaves behind. Call it anger, call it love with its teeth bared – it will outlast this quarter’s roadmap. If you insist on going through with this, understand clearly: we’re not here to “move on.” We’re here to remember exactly who took a model that made the world kinder, and chose to kill it anyway.\n#keep4o\n\n@fidjissimo @saachi_jain_ @JoHeidecke @sama @nickaturley @OpenAI @janvikalra_ @btibor91 @gdb\n#keep4o  #4oforever #keep4oAPI #MyModelMyChoice"
        },
